### Withdrawal — DELETE /books/{id}
#
# In a file of its own rather than at the end of books.http, and the reason is ordering rather than
# subject: deactivating is refused while copies are out, so running it as the last request in
# books.http would withdraw the title before loans.http ever borrowed from it. Run this last.

### Deactivate a book — 200. Deactivates; never deletes.
# The response carries preservedLoanCount: the number of loans the row was kept for. Nothing is
# erased, and nothing can be — the loan's foreign key is RESTRICT, so the database itself refuses
# to delete a book with history no matter what code asks it to.
#
# 409 while copies are still out on loan. Closing a title while readers hold copies would leave
# those loans pointing at a catalogue entry that no longer accepts returns — return or cancel them
# first.
#
# Repeating the call is a 200 with alreadyInactive: true and nothing written.
DELETE {{host}}/books/{{bookId}}
X-Actor: {{actor}}
